{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15568 - PROTEIN:5837",
  "verbalization": "Fructose 1,6-diphosphate is implicated in the target relationship with SN12K1 through a series of molecular pathways involving sialyltransferases. The evidence suggests that Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate acts as a substrate for CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which is a key enzyme in the synthesis of sialic acid-containing glycoconjugates. This enzyme is then targeted by 2-methylheptyl propanoate, another compound that appears in multiple pathways as a modulator of the same target. The presence of multiple pathways pointing to the same target enzyme and its downstream effect on SN12K1 indicates a potential regulatory or functional link between Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate and SN12K1. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate in influencing the activity of the sialyltransferase, which in turn affects SN12K1. The evidence supports a mechanism where Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate may modulate the activity of the sialyltransferase, thereby influencing SN12K1, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
